WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:15.030
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I think we will move on quite
quickly from St Luke, if we may, to something

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:20.310
that I think he would have approved of, which
is personalised medicine. Of course, St Luke

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:26.650
was a physician. I hope that is helpful, Bruce,
as a segue into the next paper.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.240
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: Thank you, Chairman. Since
you want to take this back into the Dark Ages,

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:46.789
perhaps I could start with a remark from Darwin,
who in many ways spawned the interest in genetics

00:00:46.789 --> 00:00:50.539
and what have you. He said that it is not
the strongest of the species that survive,

00:00:50.539 --> 00:00:58.609
nor the most intelligent, but the most responsive
to change. I think that sets the scene for

00:00:58.609 --> 00:01:03.749
the discussion, because when I look at the
drivers for change in healthcare systems around

00:01:03.749 --> 00:01:09.430
the world, number one is going to be economics.
The second is going to be an interaction between

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:14.150
science and technology, particularly information
technology. Then there are a set of public,

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:19.190
professional and political expectations, and
then some demographics.

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:25.820
One of the things that strikes me is that
we can solve many of the issues that face

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:32.080
us, if we are responsive to the emerging changes
in science and information technology. England,

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:42.940
in my view, has a remarkable heritage to harvest
in this area. We have Darwin, who exposed

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:55.820
the theory of evolution in the 1850s. In the
1950s we had Rosalind Franklin, Gosling, Watson

00:01:55.820 --> 00:02:03.890
and Crick, and Wilkins discovering the structure
of DNA. And then, in the mid-1970s up in Cambridge,

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:09.709
we had Sanger really setting the basis for
the sequencing of DNA. It means we have the

00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:17.030
strongest track record of understanding of
any country in the world.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:22.920
Through Genomics England and the 100,000 Genomes
Project, we are now in a commanding position

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:28.700
to lead the world in the sequencing of the
human genome. It would be a shame not to bring

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:36.090
the benefits of that scientific endeavour
to the patients of the NHS, and there is an

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:44.200
opportunity to exploit this. By that I mean
that, in understanding the genome and its

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:52.260
relationship to human disease, in relatively
short order we can get to a place where we

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:58.430
can predict the predisposition of people to
certain diseases. That will enable prevention

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:04.220
or delay of the disease through interventions.
It will take us to more precise diagnoses.

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:08.140
By that I mean that, when I trained as a surgeon,
there were four different types of breast

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:14.819
cancer. Now there are over 40. There was just
adenocarcinoma of the lung, and now there

00:03:14.819 --> 00:03:22.200
are many different types, all defined by their
genetic sequence. Understanding that enables

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:27.740
us to target treatments in a way that is far
more precise and, as a consequence, far more

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:37.130
accurate. It reduces waste in terms of ineffective
treatments. Now, this only works - we can

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:44.319
only do this - if we have laboratories around
the country that are capable of undertaking

00:03:44.319 --> 00:03:51.800
the sort of analysis that is required. We
have made some progress on that. Through Genomics

00:03:51.800 --> 00:04:00.930
England and NHS England, we have established
11 genomics medicine centre labs. We are about

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:08.410
to go through to re-procurement of the genetic
laboratory system, and we have a unique opportunity

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:15.980
there to link those in a way that is harmonised
and efficient. We will drive that through

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:18.810
the commissioning system.

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:24.350
The other thing that we have to consider is
how we link genomic information with other

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:31.660
information about patients. That takes us
into the information technology arena, and

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:37.560
also into some strong information governance
territory, but it also takes us into a space

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:43.350
where I think we need to start thinking about
how we make much greater use of mobile technology,

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:49.160
not only for this highly specific information
but for the exchange of healthcare information

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:52.660
more generally.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:59.280
With respect to the role of genomics, which
this personalised medicine strategy paper

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:07.520
is about, the third issue that we really have
to address is how we train staff to analyse,

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:19.000
understand and effect the emerging results
of this sort of endeavour. This paper is really

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:25.710
a placeholder which asks for the Board's approval
for us to go away and undertake a focused

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:32.430
piece of work to try and develop a five-year
strategy for the NHS about how we might exploit

00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:43.050
the emerging science and technology, and come
back with emerging thoughts for that strategy

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.070
in the relatively near future.

00:05:45.070 --> 00:05:50.750
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Bruce, for that
introduction. Let me invite comments and questions

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:52.800
from members of the Board.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:06:15.300
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: There is 
a notion that this is more about the ethical
and access issues. For instance, how do we

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:22.889
know that access to this amazing technology,
across the diseases that affect different

00:06:22.889 --> 00:06:31.440
people, different races and different social
classes, will be addressed with equality?

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:35.540
How do we avoid the inverse care law, basically?
How do we ensure that we avoid the inverse

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:38.840
care law as we bring in this new technology?

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:44.110
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: We avoid it at this point by
raising the question and writing it into the

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:46.669
way we solve the problem.

00:06:46.669 --> 00:06:53.110
&gt;&gt;Victor Adebowale: It seems to me that, when
you do your piece of work, I would be interested

00:06:53.110 --> 00:07:01.350
in knowing that that is part of our role in
rolling this out; that we seek to address

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:07.900
the inverse care law in our approach. It was
just a bit missing.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:14.810
&gt;&gt;John Burn: To comment and address that,
in a sense, my professional involvement in

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:19.090
this has been lifelong, so I am excited to
have it discussed here. I think the answer

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:24.440
is that the genomic medicine centres are being
built upon a very-long-established network

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:31.530
of regional genetic services. They are extrapolating
out into their local medical communities.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:35.780
The answer is that we have been doing this
for a long time, but now it is just expanding

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:41.180
in its impact. The reassurance I would have
for the Board is that the one anxiety we have

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:45.490
when we start talking about personalised medicine
- in a sense, it is under your comment - is

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:50.340
that we end up with an increasing demand,
with more and more people wanting themselves

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:56.190
tested and so on. This is where the NHS will
actually come into its own, because it is

00:07:56.190 --> 00:08:01.060
very obvious that having a system free at
the point of need makes it possible for us

00:08:01.060 --> 00:08:07.100
to deploy this sort of technology in an acceptable
way. The fears about data management and so

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:12.470
on are far less in our country than in most
other countries. That puts us in a very strong

00:08:12.470 --> 00:08:13.270
position.

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:19.370
The other reassurance I know because there
has been one very good study from the health

00:08:19.370 --> 00:08:25.900
economists in Essex, which shows that predictive
testing in colon cancer, where I have my main

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:32.849
interest, has been demonstrably shown to be
a cost saver. If you can identify and target

00:08:32.849 --> 00:08:39.060
and specifically treat tumours according to
their underlying cause, then you both prevent

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:43.659
and treat more effectively. This is a cost-saving
intervention. We should not underestimate

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:48.360
the disruptive impact of introducing it, because
of the informatics and everything else, but

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:54.420
it is worthwhile and it will save money once
it is in place.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:09:04.450
&gt;&gt;Noel Gordon: I wholly support Bruce's initiative
to try and bring a strategy together for personalised

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:11.480
medicine. While it is, crucially, genomics,
it is also much more than genomics, as you

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:16.170
said. I support it for many reasons. One is
that there are a number of initiatives already

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:22.180
under way that are related to this, not least
the accelerated access review that the Government

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:28.890
have commissioned, plus our own incubators,
which are a number of start-ups that are dealing

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:37.350
with parts of this. In that review, what role
do you see for the AHSNs? I am particularly

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:43.560
thinking of whether they can take more responsibility
and more leadership in the system around how

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:50.470
we deliver personalised medicine into the
population. There are two sides to the coin:

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:57.440
the design side and the delivery side. I am
really interested in how we think proposals

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:04.010
will come forward for making a difference
to the population, once we have invented the

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:06.750
clever drugs and devices.

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:14.540
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: For those who are not familiar
with the AHSNs, there are 15 Academic Health

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:20.220
Science Networks around the country. They
are geographically disposed in such a way

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:30.490
that they encompass particular sets of patient
flows. They contain a particular set of patient

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:36.930
flows. The idea with the Academic Health Science
Networks was that they would become an innovative

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:47.810
engine for the NHS and bring together people
with interests in healthcare, whether it be

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:54.670
providers, commissioners, local authorities
or industry, and so on and so forth. We see

00:10:54.670 --> 00:11:02.800
them as powerful agents for change. The issue
for this particular endeavour is that not

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:08.630
all Academic Health Science Networks are the
same. Different networks will have different

00:11:08.630 --> 00:11:15.600
focuses, but there are some that would be
very well equipped to help roll out personalised

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:22.529
medicine, in the sense that they have a greater
grasp of genomics and genetic medicine than

00:11:22.529 --> 00:11:31.260
others. I think that would be part of the
deliberations that we would go through, hoping

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:34.250
that the Board will approve this paper.

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:41.420
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: In my world, this would be
a classic case of test and learn: try lots

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:50.740
of things, test them, see what we can learn
and stop things quickly; so fail fast. The

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:56.399
question is whether we have that type of mentality,
but also whether we have the capability and

00:11:56.399 --> 00:12:01.649
resources to allow us to really understand
and capture that learning and disseminate

00:12:01.649 --> 00:12:06.410
it quickly, which is as much about behaviour
as it is about resources and capability.

00:12:06.410 --> 00:12:17.610
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: I think this is so new that
we can easily write in the 'fail fast', and

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:23.149
learn as quickly as we can. I do not see that
as a particular issue, particularly if we

00:12:23.149 --> 00:12:31.740
are using AHSNs and other small geographical
areas for testing. I think that resources

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:36.980
required is an issue for future deliberation.
The aim of this particular paper was simply

00:12:36.980 --> 00:12:42.019
to seek permission from the Board to proceed
with a series of work streams, understanding

00:12:42.019 --> 00:12:44.890
that the resourcing would be one.

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:50.720
&gt;&gt;Ed Smith: I did think about whether I should
register a conflict of interest at the start

00:12:50.720 --> 00:12:56.870
of the meeting with my new role, Malcolm,
but I decided that it was a mutuality of interest.

00:12:56.870 --> 00:13:01.709
This is an area where it is indeed. As I have
started the new job, I have looked across

00:13:01.709 --> 00:13:09.700
where collaboration is working well. It is
working really well with medical directors

00:13:09.700 --> 00:13:15.370
of Monitor and TDA, thanks to you and others,
Bruce. It would be really helpful to make

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:21.510
sure that my new colleagues are heavily engaged
in this, representing the communities that

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:26.779
they represent with you going forward, so
I hope that is the case.

00:13:26.779 --> 00:13:32.959
&gt;&gt;Bruce Keogh: That would be fantastic, and
it will become the case.

00:13:32.959 --> 00:13:33.890
&gt;&gt;Ed Smith: Thank you.

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:41.170
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I think it is necessary to
understand that this is not England heading

00:13:41.170 --> 00:13:46.470
off on its own. This is a global phenomenon
- the whole move towards personalised medicine,

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:52.339
or precision medicine as it is sometimes called,
and the relationship between whole-genome

00:13:52.339 --> 00:13:59.920
sequencing and other occurring and developing
technologies. I take David's point about it

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:06.709
being test and learn. In fact, the 100,000
Genome Project is very much in that territory,

00:14:06.709 --> 00:14:11.290
and it will take us some while to work out
what we have learnt from it. From our perspective,

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:22.399
we have always seen it not as a research project,
which it is in part, but actually as an NHS

00:14:22.399 --> 00:14:29.589
transformation project, and how we can maintain
an important lead in the world in developing

00:14:29.589 --> 00:14:35.010
genomic medicine as a transformation of the
way in which medicine is practised. In that

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:40.839
space, I think the work of the 11 genomic
medicine centres that we have designated is

00:14:40.839 --> 00:14:42.550
absolutely critical.

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:47.970
I think it is important that the Board understands
just how difficult this has been, for two

00:14:47.970 --> 00:14:54.930
or three reasons. One is that this is really
leading-edge stuff, and even just getting

00:14:54.930 --> 00:15:01.880
the samples for delivery for sequencing has
proved quite complicated. This is the first

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:06.899
time it has been done on this scale. For rare
diseases it had been a bit more straightforward,

00:15:06.899 --> 00:15:15.139
but for cancers it is still quite challenging.
We are not quite where we would wish to be

00:15:15.139 --> 00:15:22.410
on the volume of samples, and a lot of work
has been put in to try to develop it. I should

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:28.980
like today to give credit to the GMCs for
what they are doing, but urge a little more

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:30.709
speeding up in the submission of samples.

00:15:30.709 --> 00:15:36.240
Secondly, it is not just the technology. It
is the culture change that this has started

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:44.940
to introduce across the GMCs, and more broadly
infecting the work of the NHS providers. It

00:15:44.940 --> 00:15:51.300
is the concentration on deep phenotyping,
providing clinical data without which the

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:57.529
genotypic data is not of great value, and
then the capacity through the 100,000 Genomes

00:15:57.529 --> 00:16:05.600
Project to look at large numbers and be able
to spot patterns and learn from that. It really

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:11.639
is quite transformational, but as always the
technology's importance starts to subside

00:16:11.639 --> 00:16:17.459
as the cost of the technology also starts
to subside, and it is the practice of medicine

00:16:17.459 --> 00:16:23.930
and the cultural change that flows from it
that I think will be of great value to us.

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:27.910
Questions have been raised about the cost
impact of all this, and whether it will save

00:16:27.910 --> 00:16:36.470
us money. One always has to be cautious about
new technologies in medicine and how one assesses

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:45.199
the return, but if we can succeed in the way
the paper suggests, in heightening prediction

00:16:45.199 --> 00:16:50.339
and prevention, then there are significant
gains to be had. I think John made that point

00:16:50.339 --> 00:16:52.720
very effectively.

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:59.060
I commend the paper to the Board and ask you
to agree the principles that it proposes,

00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:04.519
and to endorse the proposal that work be taken
forward to develop a personalised medicine

00:17:04.519 --> 00:17:05.539
strategy for the NHS.

00:17:05.539 --> 00:17:07.139
Happy to do that?

00:17:07.139 --> 00:17:08.750
Thank you very much.

